## Problem

Design a car rental service. Customer A books CAR17 from 09:00 to 11:00. The branch needs one hour to inspect and prepare it, so customer B can book it from 12:00.

Now A is late. At 12:00 B arrives, but CAR17 is still on the road. The software must preserve B's reservation without pretending it can hand out a car that has not returned. It must also reject two customers trying to reserve the same time and prevent a delayed return message from releasing somebody else's later rental.

## Requirements

I will choose one branch and allocate a specific vehicle when a reservation is confirmed:

- Search by category and time range. A quote does not reserve a car; confirmation rechecks availability.
- Rental time is `[start,end)`. Add one hour of preparation after the planned return when checking future reservations.
- New reservations start now or later, last at most 30 days, and use an immutable quote valid for five minutes.
- The example tariff is ₹1,800 per started 24-hour planned period, plus ₹200 per started late hour after the booked return. Early return keeps the booked base price. Store whole paise.
- Pickup is allowed from the booked start up to, but not including, 30 minutes later. Recheck eligibility, physical ownership, maintenance and preparation completion at handover.
- Before pickup, cancellation is free before the start; at or after the start it costs ₹500. A ready vehicle left uncollected after the grace period can be cancelled as a no-show. Operator unreadiness is not a customer no-show and permits free cancellation.
- After pickup, close an actual rental through return rather than cancelling its history. Record odometer, fuel and condition. A damaged vehicle needs maintenance.
- Retries retain reservation and command identities. A late return or timer for R1 cannot release a vehicle currently held by R2.

Customers pay at the counter against an invoice in this first design. Deposits, payment networks, tax calculation, fuel/damage pricing, one-way returns, category-only promises and reservation modification are outside the implementation. Eligibility is a trusted adapter's boolean result; the Java example does not verify a real licence or identity.

## 1. Start with the car, then separate the promise from the visit

Three IDs mean different things:

| ID | Meaning | Lifetime |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CAR17 | The physical asset | Remains the same across customers. |
| R1 | A's reservation for particular dates and price | Remains in history after return or cancellation. |
| A/R1 | A's actual rental agreement | Created only when the car is handed over. |

A reservation promises future access. The agreement records what actually happened: pickup time, starting inspection and return evidence. It is possible to have a confirmed reservation while the car is not currently ready, which is exactly B's late-return problem.

The selected lifecycle is `CONFIRMED → ACTIVE → RETURNED`, or `CONFIRMED → CANCELLED`. `ACTIVE` means the customer actually has the car. It is not inferred from the clock reaching the start time.

[![Rental service checks Vehicle readiness, allocates a Reservation interval with a frozen Quote, creates an Agreement at pickup and assesses an Invoice at return.](designs/lld-6.svg)](designs/lld-6.svg)

## 2. Give each object a clear job

| Type | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `Vehicle` | Category, current active reservation, actual ready-at time and maintenance flag. |
| `Window` | Valid planned start/end and interval overlap including preparation. |
| `Quote` | Bind customer, vehicle, exact dates, tariff, base amount and expiry. |
| `Reservation` | Own the accepted quote and legal reservation transitions. |
| `AgreementView` | Keep actual pickup/return times and inspection readings under a distinct agreement identity. |
| `Invoice` | Preserve the assessed base, late or cancellation amounts once. It is not a payment receipt. |
| `Service` | Coordinate the vehicle schedule, ownership and command replay boundary. |

Category is an enum here: Economy and SUV are data used for search. A factory and a subclass for every category would not solve an interval race. Introduce different vehicle behavior only when the product requires it. Likewise the selected tariff is an immutable value; a pricing interface becomes useful when genuinely different charging algorithms must coexist.

These invariants carry the design:

- Confirmed/active promises for one vehicle do not overlap their planned blocking intervals.
- A vehicle has at most one active physical rental. Handover changes both the reservation and vehicle owner together.
- A quote cannot silently change customer, dates or price after acceptance.
- A returned/cancelled reservation remains historical; replaying it cannot mutate the vehicle's new owner.
- Actual return readiness is separate from the planned promise. A late car creates a visible conflict, not permission to invent another car.

## 3. Availability is an interval check, not AVAILABLE=true

For A's 09:00–11:00 rental, block `[09:00,12:00)`: two hours on rent followed by one hour of preparation. A proposed 11:00 pickup overlaps that interval. A 12:00 pickup touches its end and is allowed as a future promise.

Two half-open blocking intervals overlap when:

```text
existing.start < requested.blockedUntil
and requested.start < existing.blockedUntil

blockedUntil = plannedReturn + 1 hour preparation
```

Strict `<` matters. Using `<=` would reject the valid 12:00 boundary. Checking only whether one start lies inside the other interval would miss a requested interval that surrounds an existing booking.

![A's rental ends at 11:00, but its blocking interval extends through preparation until 12:00. An 11:00 pickup overlaps; 12:00 does not. Reservation confirmation checks and inserts under one authority.](learning-figures/lld-6-intervals.svg)

The example scans the selected vehicle's blocking reservations, which is simple and correct for a small branch. An interval tree or a carefully maintained ordered schedule can improve lookup when measured history justifies it. A single arbitrary predecessor lookup is not a general overlap algorithm unless its non-overlap and boundary assumptions have been proved.

### Quote first; allocate atomically

The server creates a quote and stores it. The caller later submits the quote ID, not a price it calculated. Confirmation checks the owner, expiry, current eligibility, dates and schedule again, then records the reservation and replay identity together:

```java
public synchronized ReservationView reserve(String requestId, String customerId, String quoteId) {
    key(requestId); key(customerId);
    RequestKey key=new RequestKey(customerId,requestId); SavedRequest saved=requests.get(key);
    if (saved != null) {
        if (!saved.quoteId().equals(quoteId)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Request ID payload conflict");
        return view(booking(saved.reservationId()));
    }
    Quote quote=Objects.requireNonNull(quotes.get(quoteId),"Unknown quote");
    if (!quote.customerId().equals(customerId)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Quote belongs to another customer");
    requireEligible(customerId);
    if (!now().isBefore(quote.validUntil())) throw new IllegalStateException("Quote expired");
    validateWindow(quote.window());
    if (!free(asset(quote.vehicleId()),quote.window())) throw new IllegalStateException("Vehicle interval already allocated or not ready");
    Booking booking=new Booking("R" + ++reservationSequence,quote);
    bookings.put(booking.id,booking); requests.put(key,new SavedRequest(quoteId,booking.id));
    return view(booking);
}
```

The replay key is scoped to the customer. A retry with the same key and quote refers to the same reservation, even after the quote expires. A different quote under that key is a conflict. Read the returned current reservation state; replay does not create another future promise.

A quote is deliberately not a temporary inventory hold. Two customers can obtain quotes for the same car. One confirmation wins the schedule check; the other must select another vehicle or interval. The Java lock coordinates one process only. A durable multi-server implementation must serialize all allocation writers for that vehicle and commit the reservation plus request identity together.

## 4. Recheck physical readiness at pickup and return

At pickup, require the customer and reservation, then check the chosen grace window. Only a ready, non-maintenance car with no active owner may be handed over. A retry returns `REPLAYED`; it does not authorize another physical handover.

```java
public synchronized PickupResult pickup(String reservationId, String customerId, Reading reading) {
    Booking booking=owned(reservationId,customerId); Objects.requireNonNull(reading);
    if (booking.agreement != null) {
        if (!booking.agreement.pickup().equals(reading)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Pickup payload conflict");
        return new PickupResult(Handover.REPLAYED,booking.agreement);
    }
    if (booking.state != State.CONFIRMED) throw new IllegalStateException("Reservation cannot be picked up");
    requireEligible(customerId); Instant now=now(); Window window=booking.quote.window();
    if (now.isBefore(window.start())) return new PickupResult(Handover.TOO_EARLY,null);
    if (!now.isBefore(window.start().plus(PICKUP_GRACE))) return new PickupResult(Handover.WINDOW_CLOSED,null);
    Vehicle vehicle=asset(booking.quote.vehicleId());
    if (vehicle.activeId != null || vehicle.maintenance || now.isBefore(vehicle.readyAt))
        return new PickupResult(Handover.NOT_READY,null);
    if (reading.damaged()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Do not hand over a damaged vehicle");
    booking.agreement=new AgreementView("A/" + booking.id,now,reading,null,null);
    booking.state=State.ACTIVE; vehicle.activeId=booking.id;
    return new PickupResult(Handover.STARTED,booking.agreement);
}
```

When the customer returns, the reservation ID identifies the borrowing visit. Verify its active ownership and odometer, preserve the return evidence and invoice, then release that exact owner. Preparation runs until **actual return + one hour**. Early return can therefore release future inventory after actual preparation while still honoring the quoted base price.

```java
public synchronized Invoice returnVehicle(String reservationId, String customerId, Reading reading) {
    Booking booking=owned(reservationId,customerId); Objects.requireNonNull(reading);
    if (booking.agreement == null) throw new IllegalStateException("No rental agreement");
    if (booking.state == State.RETURNED) {
        if (!booking.agreement.returned().equals(reading)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Return payload conflict");
        return booking.invoice; // A replay never changes the vehicle's newer active reservation.
    }
    Vehicle vehicle=asset(booking.quote.vehicleId());
    if (booking.state != State.ACTIVE || !booking.id.equals(vehicle.activeId)) throw new IllegalStateException("Stale rental owner");
    if (reading.odometerKm() < booking.agreement.pickup().odometerKm()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Odometer moved backwards");
    Instant now=now();
    if (now.isBefore(booking.agreement.pickedUpAt())) throw new IllegalStateException("Return before pickup");
    Duration late=now.isAfter(booking.quote.window().end()) ? Duration.between(booking.quote.window().end(),now) : Duration.ZERO;
    long latePaise=Math.multiplyExact(startedUnits(late,Duration.ofHours(1)),booking.quote.tariff().lateHourPaise());
    long base=booking.quote.basePaise();
    Invoice invoice=new Invoice("invoice/"+booking.id,booking.id,base,latePaise,0,Math.addExact(base,latePaise),now);
    booking.agreement=new AgreementView(booking.agreement.id(),booking.agreement.pickedUpAt(),booking.agreement.pickup(),now,reading);
    booking.invoice=invoice; booking.state=State.RETURNED;
    vehicle.activeId=null; vehicle.readyAt=now.plus(PREPARATION); vehicle.maintenance=reading.damaged();
    return invoice;
}
```

An old R1 return first finds R1's saved return and invoice. It does not clear whichever reservation happens to own CAR17 now. A return with different readings under that completed identity is a conflict requiring an explicit correction process, rather than overwriting the evidence.

### Follow the late return to its next customer

| Event | Actual owner/readiness | Reservation consequence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A collects at 09:00 | R1 owns CAR17 | B's R2 still promises pickup at 12:00. |
| 12:00: A has not returned | Still R1; readiness unknown | B gets `NOT_READY`. R2 remains confirmed and visibly at risk. |
| A returns at 12:10 | No active owner; ready at 13:10 | The original 12:00 promise cannot be honored. |
| 12:30: no-show worker checks R2 | Operator preparation still conflicts | Do not classify B as a customer no-show. |
| B chooses cancellation | No new handover | Cancel R2 for ₹0 because the operator was not ready. |

![A's late return leaves CAR17 physically owned by R1 when B arrives. Return at 12:10 moves readiness to 13:10. B's promise stays visible, and company unreadiness cannot produce a customer no-show fee.](learning-figures/lld-6-readiness.svg)

This solution chooses a complete remedy—free cancellation—rather than claiming to find a replacement automatically. A replacement feature would need another vehicle allocation transaction and the customer's acceptance of the changed asset.

[Open the rental timeline walkthrough](learning-labs/rental.html) to step through preparation boundaries, this late-return conflict, or an old return arriving after B's pickup. Each state is exported by the Java example and has a text transcript.

## 5. Price and cancellation boundaries are business rules

The booked base uses **started 24-hour durations**, not calendar dates:

| Planned duration | Periods | Base at ₹1,800/period |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Exactly 24 hours | 1 | ₹1,800 |
| 24 hours plus 1 nanosecond | 2 | ₹3,600 |
| 49 hours | 3 | ₹5,400 |

After the planned end, lateness uses started hours. Exactly one late hour costs ₹200; one hour plus a fraction costs ₹400. A's two-hour booked rental returning 70 minutes late costs ₹1,800 base + 2×₹200 = ₹2,200.

Do not truncate fractional seconds before rounding. The helper uses quotient and remainder against the original Duration:

```java
public static long startedUnits(Duration elapsed, Duration unit) {
    if (elapsed.isNegative() || unit.isNegative() || unit.isZero()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid duration");
    long whole=elapsed.dividedBy(unit);
    return elapsed.minus(unit.multipliedBy(whole)).isZero() ? whole : Math.incrementExact(whole);
}
```

Java's [Duration API](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/time/Duration.html) defines a duration day as exactly 24 hours. This policy therefore differs from local calendar-day charging around daylight-saving changes. Store instants for ordering and use the branch zone when displaying them. Opening hours and a calendar-day tariff would be additional explicit rules.

### Cancellation has its own cutoff

Before the start, cancellation costs zero. At the start or later, but before pickup, it costs ₹500 unless the operator cannot provide the vehicle. At the 30-minute pickup deadline, the customer may no longer start that reservation; a matching confirmed no-show can be cancelled once. After pickup, use return rather than deleting the visit.

```java
public synchronized Invoice cancel(String reservationId, String customerId) {
    Booking booking=owned(reservationId,customerId);
    if (booking.state == State.CANCELLED) return booking.invoice;
    if (booking.state != State.CONFIRMED) throw new IllegalStateException("After pickup, return the vehicle instead");
    long fee=now().isBefore(booking.quote.window().start()) || readinessIssue(booking)!=null
        ? 0 : booking.quote.tariff().lateCancelPaise();
    booking.invoice=new Invoice("cancel/"+booking.id,booking.id,0,0,fee,fee,now());
    booking.state=State.CANCELLED; return booking.invoice;
}
public synchronized boolean expireNoShow(String reservationId) {
    Booking booking=booking(reservationId);
    if (booking.state != State.CONFIRMED || now().isBefore(booking.quote.window().start().plus(PICKUP_GRACE))
        || readinessIssue(booking)!=null) return false; // Operator unreadiness is not a customer no-show.
    cancel(booking.id,booking.quote.customerId()); return true;
}
```

The no-show job acts on its reservation ID and still requires `CONFIRMED`. A delayed job for an active, returned or cancelled R1 cannot release R2. A damaged return sets maintenance; clearing that flag after repair does not erase the independent preparation deadline.

These invoices exclude taxes, fuel and damage charges. Those readings are preserved, but inventing an adjustment without an agreed rule or inspection evidence would be wrong. Payment collection would have its own receipt and retry identity. A payment timeout must not reopen the rental or erase the recorded physical return.

