java -cp examples/learning-java/out kb.rental.RentalDemo
java -cp examples/learning-java/out kb.rental.RentalChecks
```

The source implements search, quote, confirmation, pickup, return, cancellation, no-show expiry and maintenance completion. The checks exercise nested/adjacent intervals, preparation, quote expiry/replay, frozen rates, nanosecond pricing boundaries, early/late returns, stale owner events, operator fault and two competing confirmations. The clock is injected for reproducible examples; this is a local mechanism, not a durable database, licence integration or production clock-health test.

## 6. Interviewer follow-ups

**Could we promise a category instead of a particular car?** Yes, but then the allocation fact is capacity over time for the category, followed by later asset assignment. Counting today's free cars cannot prove capacity over every future interval. Specific-vehicle allocation makes the first solution easier to verify and makes the next customer's affected asset explicit.

**How would modification work?** Only for a confirmed reservation under a stated cutoff. Calculate a new quote, lock the affected vehicle schedule, exclude the reservation's own old interval when checking its replacement, and commit the new interval and quote together. Failure preserves the old allocation. Moving between vehicles needs both allocation authorities coordinated; cancelling first and hoping the replacement succeeds would lose the original promise. Modification is not exposed in this implementation.

**Can we use a database constraint for overlap?** Some databases support range/exclusion constraints; others need a different transaction design. One simple durable adapter locks the selected vehicle's schedule authority before checking and inserting, with every writer following that protocol. Request replay, cancellation and availability changes must use that same boundary. A Java lock on each web server does not coordinate those servers.

**Is a late return solved by extending R1's end?** No. That would hide R2's existing promise. Keep planned allocation and actual ownership separate, record the readiness conflict and apply the chosen remedy. More sophisticated replacement or compensation must be its own recorded operation.

**Why not use the vehicle's last drop-off branch for one-way search?** The last reservation in storage is not necessarily the relevant one for the requested time. One-way rentals require a time-ordered location plan, transfer/preparation constraints and conflict handling when an earlier trip changes. We deliberately keep same-branch return here.

**What survives a process crash?** Nothing in the memory-only example. A durable adapter stores quote terms, reservation/command identity, actual handover/return evidence and invoice together at their transition boundaries. It must recover the current physical owner before allowing another handover. A payment system cannot be used as the inventory authority.

## 7. Closing answer

“I would reserve a specific vehicle against a half-open interval that includes preparation, using a frozen quote and one atomic allocation check. Pickup separately verifies the actual car is ready and creates an agreement. Return records the visit and price once, then releases only that visit's ownership and starts actual preparation. A late car makes later promises visibly at risk; the chosen remedy is free cancellation rather than a fictional handover or customer no-show fee. Durable adapters must preserve those same allocation and identity boundaries.”
